    "lawCode": "Amd §4325, Ins L; amd §4406-c, Pub Health L; add §6826-a, Ed L",
    "lawSection": "Insurance Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S4786\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the insurance law, the public health\nlaw and the education law, in relation to copayments for prescription\ndrugs\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL : To reduce consumers' out-of-pocket\nprescription drug costs by prohibiting copayments which exceed the\ncost of the prescribed drug.\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS : Section one adds a new subsection\n(g) to section 4325 of the Insurance Law to prohibit insurers offering\nprescription drug benefits from requiring or permitting copayments\nwhich exceed the usual and customary cost of the prescribed drug_\n\nSection two adds a new subdivision 6 to section 4406-c of the Public\nHealth Law to make the same change for health maintenance\norganizations (HMOs).\n\nSection three adds a new section 6826-a to the Education Law to\nrequire pharmacies to charge consumers the lesser of the copayment\nrequired by a health plan and the pharmacy's usual customary and\nreasonable price for that drug.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :  Many health plans have adopted multi-tier copayment\nstructures for pharmacy benefits in an effort to control prescription\ndrug costs.  Typically, these systems involve a low copayment for\ngeneric drugs, a higher copayment for name-brand drugs that are\n\"preferred\" by the plan and a third and much higher copayment for\nname-brand \"non-preferred\" drugs. While this effort to control rapidly\nescalating prescription drug costs through multi-tier copayment\nsystems is understandable, these cost control efforts can unfairly\npenalize some consumers by requiring them to make a copayment that\nmight actually exceed the cost of the drug that has been prescribed\nfor them. A recent Wall Street Journal article, for example, detailed\nhow consumers in Boston were making $5 copayments at a local chain\npharmacy for a generic heart drug, when the pharmacy benefit manager\nhad in fact negotiated a 76 cents price for the drug and was pocketing\nthe difference. This legislation would end this inequity by\nprohibiting insurers and health plans from requiring the higher\ncopayments, and requiring pharmacies to change the lesser of the\ncopayment or the usual and customary price for the drug. At a time\nwhen consumers and employer groups are reeling from escalating drug\nexpenditures, this measure will provide some relief by reducing\nconsumers' out-of-pocket costs.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS : None,\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE : Ninety days after it shall become a law.\n",
