    "lawCode": "Amd §4325, Ins L; amd §4406-c, Pub Health L; add §6826-a, Ed L",
    "lawSection": "Insurance Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S4786A\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\nAn act to amend the insurance law, the public health law and the\neducation law, in relation to copayments for prescription drugs\n\nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\nTo reduce consumers' out-of-pocket prescription drug costs by\nprohibiting copayments which exceed the cost of the prescribed drug.\n\nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\nSection one adds a new subsection (h) to section 4325 of the Insurance\nLaw to prohibit insurers offering prescription drug benefits from\nrequiring or permitting copayments which exceed the usual and\ncustomary cost of the prescribed drug.\n\nSection two adds a new subdivision 6 to section 4406-c of the Public\nHealth Law to make the same change for health maintenance\norganizations (HMOs).\n\nSection three adds a new section 6826-a to the Education Law to\nrequire pharmacies to charge consumers the lesser of the copayment\nrequired by a health plan and the pharmacy's usual customary and\nreasonable price for that drug.\n\nSection four sets out the effective date.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\nMany health plans have adopted multi-tier copayment structures for\npharmacy benefits in an effort to control prescription drug costs.\nTypically, these systems involve a low copayment for generic drugs, a\nhigher copayment for name-brand drugs that are \"preferred\" by the plan\nand a third and much higher copayment for name-brand \"non-preferred\"\ndrugs. While this effort to control rapidly escalating prescription\ndrug costs through multi-tier copayment systems is understandable,\nthese cost control efforts can unfairly penalize some consumers by\nrequiring them to make a copayment that might actually exceed the cost\nof the drug that has been prescribed for them.  A recent Wall Street\nJournal article, for example, detailed how consumers in Boston were\nmaking $5 copayments at a local chain pharmacy for a generic heart\ndrug, when the pharmacy benefit manager had in fact negotiated a 76\ncents price for the drug and was pocketing the difference.\n\nThis legislation would end this inequity by prohibiting insurers and\nhealth plans from requiring the higher copayments, and requiring\npharmacies to change the lesser of the copayment or the usual and\ncustomary price for the drug. At a time when consumers and employer\ngroups are reeling from escalating drug expenditures, this measure\nwill provide some relief by reducing consumers' out-of-pocket costs.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\nSimilar provisions contained in legislation\n(A.11235) passed in 2000.\n2001: A.6483  Referred to insurance\n2002: A.6483  Passed Assembly\n2003: A.6851A Passed Assembly\n\n\n2004: A.6851A Passed Assembly\n2005: A.4406  Referred to Insurance\n2006: A.4406  Referred to Insurance\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\nNone.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nNinety days after it shall become a law.\n\n",
